 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T05:05+08:00",
 "answer": "I can't pull a true live reading, and the sources I found were cached at different times, so treat these numbers as approximate. They suggest air quality in Penang is generally **moderate**, with occasional readings that are worse for sensitive groups.\n\n| Source | Location | US AQI | Category |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Weather Underground (Copernicus data) | Penang | 68 | Moderate; PM2.5 is the main pollutant |\n| AccuWeather | Penang International Airport | 67 | Listed as \"Poor\" on their own scale |\n| IQAir | Bukit Mertajam | 58 | Moderate (PM2.5 about 12.9 µg/m³) |\n| IQAir | Tanjung Tokong | 79 | Moderate |\n| IQAir | George Town | 125 | Unhealthy for sensitive groups |\n| IQAir | Batu Feringgi | 159 | Unhealthy |\n\nThe IQAir pages carry different timestamps, some from October, so the George Town and Batu Feringgi figures may be old snapshots. The spread also shows how much readings vary between stations and between data providers.\n\nPM2.5 is the dominant pollutant across the sources. Penang's air often gets worse during regional haze episodes, usually from smoke carried over from Sumatra, or during local traffic and industrial buildup.\n\n**What to do:**\n- At AQI 51–100, most people can carry on as usual. People with asthma, heart or lung conditions, as well as children and older adults, should limit long or heavy outdoor exertion.\n- Above 100, sensitive groups should cut down on time outdoors, and a mask such as an N95 can help.\n\nFor a current reading, check the IQAir page for your area. You can also use the Malaysian Department of Environment's official API (APIMS) at apims.doe.gov.my, or the MyIPU app.",
